exchange for a tithe of one falcon per year, as a sign that the land still
lay in possession of its new emperor.  The knights were so pleased by the
trivial nature of their new emperor's demand that they chose to gift him
with a falcon created of their most precious gems and purest gold, as a
sign of their esteem.

The falcon never reached its destination, stolen again and again by those
who sought its riches.
